namespace com.trustblocks.attestation@1.0.0

import org.accordproject.runtime@0.2.0.{Request, Response} from https://models.accordproject.org/accordproject/runtime@0.2.0.cto

/**
 * The standard trigger pair for "someone in office certifies something
 * about this contract" -- com.trustblocks.xtdb.certification/certify!
 * always calls execute/trigger! with an AttestationRequest, so any
 * contract wanting attestation support imports these two types and adds
 * one branch to its own stored clause, the same way
 * com.trustblocks.municipal.employment.execution@1.0.0's
 * BoardApprovalRequest/Response are one branch of that contract's clause.
 *
 * Deliberately generic and reusable, unlike a contract's own execution
 * types: BoardApprovalRequest only ever means one thing, but
 * AttestationRequest is the same shape whether the statement is "the
 * council voted to approve this" or "the survey plat was recorded" --
 * what makes an attestation valid is the officeholder's authority and
 * the contract's own state at the time, checked by the caller
 * (identity/OfficeGrant, before this ever runs) and the clause (this
 * contract's own eligibility rule) respectively. Neither the DID that
 * signs the resulting credential nor when it was actually signed
 * appears here -- a clause has no identity or clock to supply them with
 * (see com.trustblocks.clause's own docstring on why). The DID is
 * already `id` on com.trustblocks.identity@1.0.0.Attestation (every
 * VC's credentialSubject.id is its subject's DID, by that model's own
 * convention); certify! adds the wall-clock moment itself, as that
 * model's own certifiedAt, once the clause has answered.
 */

transaction AttestationRequest extends Request {
  o String statement
  o String documentHash
  o String requiredAuthority
}

transaction AttestationResponse extends Response {
  o String statement
  o String documentHash
}
